<BaseLayout title="Is the Food System Designed to Make Healthy Food Expensive?" description="Eating well costs more. It's not a market accident — it's the result of decades of agricultural policy, corporate subsidies and positioning strategy." lang="en" path="/articulos/sistema-alimentario">

  <article>

    <header class="article-header">
      <div class="header-inner">
        <span class="categoria">Food Economics</span>
        <h1>Is the Food System Designed to Make Healthy Food Expensive?</h1>
        <p class="subtitulo">Eating well costs more. It's not a market accident — it's the result of decades of agricultural policy, corporate subsidies and positioning strategy.</p>
        <div class="meta">
          <span class="autor-badge">NF</span>
          <div class="meta-text">
            <strong>NexoraFood</strong> · June 18, 2026 · 5 min read
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <div class="content">

        <p class="lead">There's a paradox that repeats itself in almost every supermarket in the world: a bag of potato chips costs less than a bag of walnuts. Frozen pizza competes against — and beats — fresh salmon. It's not a coincidence. It's design.</p>

        <p>Eating well, within the global food system as it's built today, carries an extra cost that not everyone can afford. The first step is understanding why.</p>

        <div class="h2-wrap"><h2>The gap Harvard measured</h2></div>

        <p>In 2013, researchers at the Harvard School of Public Health published the most exhaustive study to date on the price difference between healthy and unhealthy diets. Their conclusion was stark: following a healthy diet costs roughly 1.50 dollars more per day than an unhealthy one.</p>

        <div class="pull-quote">
          <p>"Multiplied across a family of four over a year, that's more than 2,000 extra dollars just to eat better. For millions of households, that isn't a matter of habits — it's an impossible decision."</p>
        </div>

        <p>A 2022 survey in the United States found that nearly half of respondents — 46% — identified the perceived high cost of healthy food as their main barrier to improving their diet. Not a lack of information. Not a lack of willpower. The price.</p>

        <div class="h2-wrap"><h2>Why healthy food costs more</h2></div>

        <ul class="article-list">
          <li><strong>Production costs.</strong> Organic farming requires more labor, has lower yields per hectare, and faces greater losses to pests.</li>
          <li><strong>Subsidies that favor ultra-processed foods.</strong> In many countries, agricultural subsidy systems have historically favored corn, soy and wheat — the raw material of ultra-processed foods. This artificially cheapens high-fructose corn syrup, refined vegetable oils and ultra-processed flours.</li>
          <li><strong>Shorter, less efficient distribution chains.</strong> Fresh produce has a limited shelf life, requires refrigeration, and is wasted more often in transit. An ultra-processed product with preservatives can travel thousands of miles without issue and last months on the shelf.</li>
          <li><strong>The "health premium" as a marketing strategy.</strong> The markup on healthy food doesn't always reflect real costs — often it reflects market positioning. "Organic," "natural," "gluten-free," "keto-friendly" are labels many brands use to charge more without there always being a nutritional difference that justifies it.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="h2-wrap"><h2>Who leads this market?</h2></div>

        <p>Whole Foods Market, owned by Amazon, held more than 15% of the organic market in 2024. And behind the "natural" and "healthy" brands are, in many cases, the same old giants: Nestlé, Danone and General Mills dominate the segment through subsidiaries and strategic acquisitions.</p>

        <div class="highlight-box">
          <p>The consumer who pays the <strong>"healthy"</strong> premium is often funding the very same corporations that also sell ultra-processed foods. <strong>"Wellness"</strong> has become one of the most profitable markets in the world precisely because demand exists but supply is concentrated.</p>
        </div>

        <p>This doesn't mean every healthy product is a marketing trap. It means the system isn't designed to democratize access to good food.</p>

        <div class="h2-wrap"><h2>What could change?</h2></div>

        <ul class="article-list">
          <li><strong>Smarter subsidy policies.</strong> Redirecting part of agricultural subsidies toward fruits, vegetables and quality protein would structurally lower their prices.</li>
          <li><strong>Retail-level price incentives.</strong> A meta-analysis published in 2024 in The Lancet Planetary Health confirmed that price reductions on fruits and vegetables consistently increase their consumption.</li>
          <li><strong>Food infrastructure in underserved communities.</strong> Bringing supermarkets and farmers' markets to areas without access doesn't solve everything on its own, but it's a necessary condition.</li>
          <li><strong>Transparency around the "health premium."</strong> Regulating what can be labeled "healthy" would prevent marketing from capturing demand without delivering real nutritional value.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="divider"></div>

        <div class="closing-box">
          <p>The price of eating well isn't a market accident. It's the result of decades of political decisions, corporate investments and subsidy structures that favored the production of cheap calories over the production of accessible nutrition.</p>
          <p><strong>"Because behind every food there is far more than calories: there are economic, political and cultural decisions that determine who can access it."</strong></p>
        </div>

        <div class="fuentes">
          <div class="fuentes-label">Sources</div>
          <p>Harvard School of Public Health · International Food Information Council Food & Health Survey 2024 · Organic Trade Association 2024 · USDA Food Access Research Atlas 2025 · The Lancet Planetary Health, 2024 · Global Market Insights, Organic Food Market Report 2024</p>
          <p class="disclaimer">This article is informational and journalistic in nature. The data cited corresponds to publicly available studies and market reports.</p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

